+# Native Cabinet account session
+
+**Status: accepted, 2026-10-09; self-profile parity amended 2026-10-10; implementation and native proof pending.**
+The user accepted the remaining technical protocol with "принимаем": the five
+operations and their exact request/response/error definitions, a code lifetime
+of 60 seconds after issuance and a 900-second native attempt lifetime. Earlier
+accepted acquisition, lifecycle, recovery, isolation, full web self-profile parity
+without phone and automatic browser continuation remain binding, including the
+documented client-impersonation risk. No account-switching workflow is added.
+Prepared within
+[01-0008-native-cabinet-account-session](../tickets/01-0008-native-cabinet-account-session.md).
+The complete profile is the accepted local authority; an accessible immutable
+publication reference and recipient implementation evidence are still pending.
+The [existing agreements](../boundaries.md#accepted-content-admission) fix Cabinet
+account ownership, separate Daychi content access and installation-scoped sign-out.
+[Scoped source evidence](../current-system.md#native-account-session-source--2026-10-08)
+explains the implementation gap; it does not establish deployed native support.
+
+## Accepted credential lifetime and renewal
+
+Decision: the user, 2026-10-08, in response to retaining Cabinet's existing
+90-day/seven-day policy for native credentials and preserving the predecessor
+after renewal. A newly issued native Cabinet bearer expires 90 days after issuance;
+it becomes renewable when its age is strictly greater than seven days. Renewal
+issues another credential for the same native session, expiring 90 days from its
+own issuance. The session has no absolute lifetime cap while eligible renewal
+continues. The previous credential remains valid until its own expiry while the
+session and account remain eligible; revocation of that session ends all of its
+credentials. This permits recovery from a lost renewal response or failed local
+write without immediately invalidating the credential still stored by the client.
+
+The accepted tradeoff is that a copied valid bearer can also renew the session;
+renewal alone does not detect theft. Credential serialization, renewal wire
+operation, revocation mechanics, callback and other profile details below are specified below. The full protocol acceptance on 2026-10-09 settles those
+definitions; it does not establish implementation/runtime capability.
+
+## Accepted native credential acquisition
+
+The external-browser authorization-code-and-PKCE method was agreed by the user
+on 2026-10-04. On 2026-10-08 the user reaffirmed it with "да" and corrected the
+assistant's repeated approval request: browser sign-in was already agreed.
+This is retained scope, not another acquisition-method checkpoint.
+
+Native Daychi uses Cabinet-owned sign-in through the external system browser.
+Cabinet returns a short-lived single-use code; Daychi exchanges it for a
+Cabinet-issued bearer using PKCE to bind the exchange to the originating attempt.
+The return is bound to that attempt and the configured Cabinet issuer.
+Cancellation or an unrelated return leaves the current session unchanged.
+Cabinet owns sign-in and issuance; existing Cabinet web access is preserved.
+The registered callback is accepted below; exact wire fields, durations and
+endpoint/error definitions are accepted below. This does not select a complete
+OAuth/OIDC profile.
+
+## Accepted local sign-out and failure policy
+
+Decision: the user, 2026-10-08, replied "принимаем" to immediate local sign-out
+without waiting for network revocation, no retained retry credential, and explicit
+incomplete sign-out on local deletion failure.
+
+Sign-out immediately disables Cabinet account use in Daychi and removes the
+locally saved Cabinet session. It attempts Cabinet revocation, but local completion
+does not wait for the network. Offline or failed revocation cannot establish remote
+invalidation; a copied credential may remain usable. The client retains no token
+for a later revocation queue. If local deletion fails, account use stays disabled
+in memory and sign-out is reported incomplete; restart persistence is not promised.
+Other clients' sessions and Daychi's independent content access remain unaffected.
+
+The exact revoke operation and retention guarantees are accepted below; the
+session-isolation decision fixes the concurrency guarantee. This acceptance
+does not establish implementation or native runtime proof.
+
+## Accepted account-block recovery
+
+Decision: the user, 2026-10-08, replied "сохраняем" to retaining the credential
+after Cabinet reports an account block, so access can recover after unblock.
+
+When Cabinet reports that the account is blocked, Daychi disables Cabinet account
+use but retains its credential. After unblock, the next successful authoritative
+check restores account use if that credential remains valid. An expired or revoked
+credential requires a new sign-in; unblock neither extends its lifetime nor reverses
+revocation. Retaining a credential never authorizes use from a cached account.
+Exact HTTP/error definitions are accepted below.
+
+## Accepted registered callback
+
+Decision: the user, 2026-10-08, replied "пойдет" to the dedicated custom-scheme
+callback after comparing it with an app-bound HTTPS link, including scheme
+collision and PKCE protection limits.
+
+Cabinet registers exactly `su.xuanxue.daychi:/oauth/cabinet` (single slash) for
+native Daychi. Daychi adds that scheme on iOS and Android while preserving existing
+`quietpractice` invitation links and package/bundle identifiers. Cabinet redirects
+only to that exact registered URI; Daychi validates the return against its current
+attempt and Cabinet issuer. This selects the school's `xuanxue.su` namespace for
+the callback; the release owner must control that namespace.
+
+Custom schemes do not guarantee exclusive delivery to Daychi: another app may
+register the same scheme. PKCE prevents that app from exchanging an intercepted
+code without the originating verifier, but does not guarantee delivery. Exact
+query-field and raw-URI validation definitions are accepted below; this
+acceptance establishes neither native configuration nor runtime conformance.
+
+## Accepted interrupted code-exchange recovery
+
+Decision: the user, 2026-10-09, replied "согласен" to a fresh browser attempt
+after a lost code-exchange result, preserving any prior usable session.
+
+After dispatching a code-exchange request, if a new credential has not been
+successfully obtained and durably stored, Daychi treats the new sign-in as
+incomplete and does not replay that exchange. It preserves any prior usable
+Cabinet session. Completing the new sign-in requires a fresh browser attempt;
+Cabinet may reuse its valid browser session without repeating authentication.
+If the new credential was durably installed, normal restoration applies.
+The same rule covers a crash before durable installation or failure to save
+the returned credential. No separate server result-recovery operation is added.
+
+This selects recovery behavior; the exact exchange wire, timing and client
+persistence representation remain outside this decision. Native proof is pending.
+
+## Accepted session isolation and revocation guarantees
+
+Decision: the user, 2026-10-09, accepted item 3 with "сессия отдельно по каждому
+интерфейсу сессии". Client interfaces have independent sessions: native Daychi
+does not share its grant with a Cabinet browser session or another installation.
+Each completed new native sign-in creates a distinct grant; renewal keeps it.
+Revoking the current grant invalidates all credentials issued for it, including
+renewed credentials, without terminating other native grants or browser sessions.
+After Cabinet acknowledges revocation, a concurrent renewal cannot leave a usable
+credential for that grant. A grant identifies a sign-in, not physical hardware.
+This decision does not add an account-switching user story or device inventory.
+Exact revocation request/response and retention guarantees are accepted below.
+
+## Accepted failure categories
+
+Decision: the user, 2026-10-09, accepted item 5. Cabinet distinguishes an invalid
+session, a blocked account and temporary unavailability. Daychi clears only the
+matching invalid Cabinet credential; it retains a blocked account's credential
+with account use disabled, and retains a credential after temporary failure.
+The existing local sign-out and account-block recovery decisions still govern.
+Exact HTTP/error codes, response validation and retry timing are accepted below.
+
+## Accepted joint verification scope
+
+Decision: the user, 2026-10-09, accepted item 6. Provider/client checks cover
+sign-in, cancellation, restoration, renewal, revocation and failures, together
+with actual sign-in through a native Daychi build against a conforming Cabinet
+provider. Joint proof on one supported native platform is sufficient; callback
+configuration and client checks cover both iOS and Android. Existing web,
+invitation, content and ordinary schedule/reminder behavior remain regression
+obligations. Expo Go or a fake provider alone cannot establish that joint proof.
+The detailed wire inputs of common cases below are part of the accepted profile.
