+## Accepted browser continuation without additional confirmation
+
+Decision: the user, 2026-10-09, instructed "убери фрикшн" after the extra
+Continue button and the client-impersonation concern were explained, and asked
+to document that risk. Cabinet uses a valid active browser session to issue the
+native authorization code and return automatically to Daychi. It adds no account
+confirmation, consent or Continue screen for this handoff. If browser sign-in is
+necessary, completion of the existing sign-in returns through the validated
+authorization transaction automatically, without a second Cabinet confirmation.
+Existing account eligibility, registered callback, state/issuer validation and
+PKCE remain binding. This removes Cabinet-added handoff friction; it does not
+change operating-system or external login-provider interaction.
+
+### Accepted risk — native client impersonation
+
+The user explicitly accepted this risk on 2026-10-09. The public `daychi-native`
+client ID and custom-scheme callback do not prove that the initiating app is
+genuine Daychi. A malicious installed app able to receive the same callback scheme
+can initiate its own request with its own PKCE verifier. With an active Cabinet
+browser session and automatic authorization, it could obtain and redeem a code
+without a Cabinet confirmation click, gaining a Cabinet native bearer for that
+account. PKCE prevents redemption of another initiator's intercepted code; it
+does not prevent an impersonator from redeeming its own transaction.
+
+A confirmation on Cabinet's genuine browser page could require a visible user
+action, rather than unnoticed issuance from an existing browser session. It would
+not authenticate the calling app: an impersonator can use the registered client
+ID and lead the user to the same Cabinet confirmation. The assistant's earlier
+wording overstated the button's protection; the user challenged it on 2026-10-09.
+The weakness is unverified native client/callback ownership, not the absence of a
+button alone. Removing the button does not remove PKCE or other accepted bindings.
+The attack requires a malicious installed app that can receive this callback;
+merely copying a client ID without obtaining the code and its verifier is insufficient.
+
+The exposed bearer can read the full self-profile and renew under the accepted
+lifecycle until expiry or grant/account refusal. This is a documented deviation
+from [RFC 8252 section 8.6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8252#section-8.6)'s
+recommendation against automatic authorization when client identity cannot be
+assured. No app-identity proof, extra confirmation or other compensating mechanism
+is claimed. This accepted tradeoff is not a finding of deployed exploitation or
+native implementation capability.
+
+## Alignment observations — 2026-10-09
+
+The user challenged the additional browser Continue click, asked for current
+Daychi consumers to establish credential/payload needs, and challenged whether
+account switching is an existing application story. These are not accepted
+requirements. Current consumer evidence is recorded in
+[current-system](../current-system.md#native-consumer-needs--2026-10-09).
+The accepted target bearer transport to both backends remains in
+[content admission](../boundaries.md#accepted-content-admission). This account-session
+slice preserves the legacy content credential; it does not deliver that admission
+bridge. An account-only token must not be represented as already authorizing
+protected Daychi content. No in-place account replacement workflow is approved.
+
+## Authority and extent
+
+Cabinet is the authorization server, account owner and resource server for this
+profile. Native Daychi is the public client `daychi-native`; it has no client secret.
+This profile permits reading the signed-in account, renewing its credential and
+revoking its native grant. Reading the full self-profile includes effective roles
+but does not grant staff API operations or Daychi content access.
+Existing Cabinet browser cookies and Daychi invitation/content credentials remain
+independent. A Cabinet credential is never sent to the existing Daychi backend.
+The future Cabinet-to-Daychi admission check is outside this profile.
+
+The [accepted session isolation](#accepted-session-isolation-and-revocation-guarantees)
+governs the grant representation. Each successful code exchange creates a distinct native authorization grant.
+`session_id` identifies that grant, not hardware, an account or every login on a
+physical device. Renewal keeps the grant; another sign-in creates another grant.
+The client stores one current Cabinet grant per configured issuer. Sign-out revokes
+the current known grant, not all grants for the account, client ID or device.
+An abandoned grant cannot be rediscovered from a device fingerprint in this profile.
+
+The configured HTTPS issuer is exactly `https://staging.xuanxue.su` for staging or
+`https://xuanxue.su` for production. It has no trailing slash or `/api` suffix.
+Operations below resolve against that issuer; credentials and account identities
+from different issuers are not interchangeable. The issuer is trusted application
+configuration, never selected by a received link. No discovery service is required.
+
+## Transport and common representation
+
+All server operations use HTTPS. Browser authorization runs in the external system
+browser. API requests use the operation's explicit credential; cookies cannot
+substitute for a native bearer or code. Native API responses neither issue nor clear
+browser cookies. Native form endpoints cannot require the browser-only CSRF header;
+their explicit bindings below protect the operation.
+
+JSON responses use `application/json`, `Cache-Control: no-store` and
+`Pragma: no-cache`; token-bearing requests/responses and authorization URLs must
+not enter application logs, analytics, crash reports or persisted navigation.
+Form POSTs use UTF-8 `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`. Duplicate parameters,
+unknown fields and wrong types are rejected. Parameter names are case sensitive.
+JSON success examples list required fields; no field is nullable. Account `email`
+and `pendingEmail` follow the web's explicit optional-field omission rules below.
+Clients require the specified fields/types and ignore additional response fields.
+
+`access_token` is opaque to Daychi, 1–1024 ASCII bytes, conforming to the bearer
+`b64token` grammar, with at least 128 bits of unpredictable credential material.
+Daychi never decodes it to infer identity or authorization. Cabinet chooses its
+representation, signing, storage and executable definitions. Cabinet must distinguish
+native grants from browser credentials and reject using either in the other's channel.
+`session_id` is a stable opaque case-sensitive string matching `[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,128}`.
+Account `id` is Cabinet's stable opaque case-sensitive identifier, 1–128 ASCII
+characters matching `[A-Za-z0-9_-]+`; consumers do not infer a database type.
+`name` is the current Cabinet display name, a Unicode string, including empty string
+when the account has no display name. The full account shape implements
+[accepted web self-profile parity](#accepted-web-self-profile-parity):
+
+| Account field | Type and meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `id` | Stable Cabinet account ID as defined above |
+| `name` | Cabinet display name as defined above |
+| `roles` | Array of effective `admin`, `teacher`, `assistant`, `accountant` roles; empty for a student and while student mode is enabled |
+| `status` | `active` or `blocked`, matching Cabinet `MeDto`; a blocked account read still returns the accepted refusal instead of a successful profile |
+| `telegramLinked` | Boolean: Telegram is linked as a login key |
+| `botChatActive` | Boolean: Cabinet has an active personal bot chat for this user |
+| `hasEmail` | Boolean: a confirmed email exists |
+| `email` | Optional string: confirmed email; omitted when absent, consistent with `hasEmail` |
+| `googleLinked` | Boolean: Google is linked as a login key |
+| `pendingEmail` | Optional string: email awaiting confirmation; omitted when absent; not a valid login key |
+| `noTelegram` | Boolean: user has indicated they do not have Telegram |
+| `needsProfile` | Boolean: user has not yet confirmed their own name |
+| `studentMode` | Boolean: Cabinet's student mode is enabled |
+| `canUseStudentMode` | Boolean: Cabinet says student mode is available; it is not inferred from effective `roles` |
+| `homeHiddenTiles` | Required array of `nextLesson`, `notice`, `exams`, `payment`, `grading`, `events`: Cabinet home tiles hidden by the account owner. Each key appears at most once, in the listed canonical order. `[]` means no hidden tiles. Cabinet returns the same normalized value as web `MeDto`; absent stored data yields `[]`, duplicate and unknown stored keys are removed. This field conveys a Cabinet preference; native account access adds no tile UI or preference mutation/synchronization. |
+
+All fields except the two explicitly optional emails are required. This shape
+matches Cabinet `shared/src/me.ts`, `shared/src/home-tiles.ts` and `toMeDto` at
+`fee65f3b8004d4ecac6c2d3da668cf966be0d0f5`, linked in the
+[2026-10-10 source check](../current-system.md#assignment-and-native-profile-recheck--2026-10-10).
+Raw Telegram/Google identifiers are not web self-profile fields.
+No phone field exists in that baseline; the user excluded adding one.
+Cabinet owns profile mapping and meanings; shared profile changes must preserve
+web/native parity through the coordinated contract revision. The account response
+does not embed a content-admission decision. JSON numeric durations are nonnegative integers in seconds. Response
+durations describe the credential supplied/returned by that operation, not the
+most recent credential issued elsewhere for the same grant.
+
+## Browser authorization and callback
+
+`GET /auth/native/authorize` is a browser entry, not a JSON API. The registered
+redirect URI is exactly `su.xuanxue.daychi:/oauth/cabinet` (single slash).
+Daychi registers that additional scheme for iOS and Android while preserving
+`quietpractice`, existing invitation routing and package/bundle identifiers.
+This implements the [accepted registered callback](#accepted-registered-callback).
+The release owner must control the scheme's domain namespace.
+
+Required query fields:
+
+| Field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `response_type` | `code` |
+| `client_id` | `daychi-native` |
+| `redirect_uri` | Exact registered URI |
+| `scope` | Exactly `account:read` |
+| `state` | Fresh random 32 bytes, base64url without padding: 43 characters |
+| `code_challenge` | S256 digest, base64url without padding: 43 characters |
+| `code_challenge_method` | `S256` |
+
+Daychi generates a fresh 32-byte random verifier encoded as 43 base64url
+characters without padding; its challenge is SHA-256 of the ASCII verifier.
+It durably stores issuer, state, verifier, redirect URI and attempt start time
+before opening the browser. Only one attempt is current; a new attempt or logout
+supersedes the old one. A local attempt expires after 900 elapsed seconds; an
+expired attempt requires a new sign-in. Clock uncertainty fails the attempt closed.
+
+Cabinet uses its existing browser login methods and account creation restrictions.
+The [accepted automatic continuation](#accepted-browser-continuation-without-additional-confirmation)
+governs this handoff: a valid active browser session returns a code automatically,
+without a Cabinet account-confirmation, consent or Continue screen. Completion
+of necessary browser sign-in also continues automatically. Browser/login
+cancellation remains possible; provider cancellation returns `access_denied`.
+Login must return to this internal authorization entry,
+preserving the validated transaction, without relaxing the existing internal-only
+`returnTo` policy. Completing an email link in another tab must leave a usable
+original authorization transaction or offer a fresh attempt; it must not return
+a code belonging to another transaction. Blocked accounts cannot receive a code.
+
+Success redirects to the registered URI with exactly `code`, `state` and `iss`.
+`code` is fresh opaque base64url, 43 characters, single-use and valid for 60 seconds
+after issuance. `state` is the original value; `iss` is the exact configured issuer.
+All values are percent encoded as query components. The code is bound to issuer,
+client, redirect URI, PKCE challenge and the selected Cabinet account. At exchange
+the current account must still exist and be active. Browser cookies never cross
+the callback. A code is valid when server time is strictly before its expiry.
+
+For an unknown client or nonmatching redirect URI, Cabinet returns a local HTTP 400
+page and never redirects. Missing/malformed state also stays on a local HTTP 400
+page. Once client, redirect and state are valid, other authorization failures return
+exactly `error`, `state`, `iss`, with error `invalid_request`,
+`unsupported_response_type`, `invalid_scope`, `access_denied`,
+`temporarily_unavailable` or `server_error`, as applicable. No diagnostic data is
+carried in a callback.
+
+Daychi receives callbacks on cold and warm starts before ordinary invite routing.
+The raw URI before `?` must equal the registered single-slash URI exactly, before
+any path/scheme normalization; authority, userinfo, port, encoded path alternatives
+and fragments are rejected. Query components are UTF-8 percent decoded exactly
+once; malformed encoding and duplicate decoded names are rejected. Check only the
+success or error field set, exact state and exact issuer against the pending attempt.
+Code/state use their declared 43-character syntax; errors use the declared callback
+allowlist. Empty values or extra fields are invalid. No URI normalization may make
+an alternative callback address eligible.
+A callback with neither/both code and error, a wrong binding or an expired attempt
+has no credential effect. Local browser cancellation clears the attempt and leaves
+an existing Cabinet session usable. The app removes sensitive callback data from
+navigation and clears the verifier after a terminal result. It never replays a
+consumed transaction. Late browser/network results after cancellation, replacement
+or logout cannot install a credential.
+
+## Code exchange
+
+`POST /api/auth/native/token` accepts exactly these form fields:
+`grant_type=authorization_code`, `client_id=daychi-native`, `redirect_uri` equal
+to the registered URI, `code`, `code_verifier`. Code and verifier are each 43
+base64url characters. No client authentication or bearer header is supplied.
+
+Cabinet validates every binding and atomically consumes a valid code. A wrong
+verifier never consumes another client's valid transaction. Exactly one concurrent
+valid exchange may succeed. Deleted/blocked accounts, expired/used/unknown codes
+and wrong bindings return HTTP 400 `{"error":"invalid_grant"}` without indicating
+which binding failed. An unsupported grant type returns HTTP 400
+`{"error":"unsupported_grant_type"}`; other malformed form requests return
+HTTP 400 `{"error":"invalid_request"}`. Unknown client IDs return HTTP 400
+`{"error":"invalid_client"}`.
+
+Success is HTTP 200 with this complete shape (credential and IDs illustrative):
+
+```json
+{
+  "access_token": "opaque-native-credential",
+  "token_type": "Bearer",
+  "expires_in": 7776000,
+  "scope": "account:read",
+  "session_id": "nativegrant123456",
+  "renew_after": 604801
+}
+```
+
+The [accepted interrupted exchange recovery](#accepted-interrupted-code-exchange-recovery)
+governs this dispatch/persistence detail. Before sending an exchange
+request, Daychi durably makes that transaction
+nonretryable. A crash/restart after dispatch before durable credential installation
+cannot send the same code again or accept a duplicate callback; it requires a
+fresh browser attempt. A durably installed credential is restored normally. Failure to
+establish that durable state prevents dispatch and preserves the prior session.
+The choice of local representation is Daychi's.
+
+No `refresh_token` or ID token is issued. This is authorization-code acquisition
+with PKCE followed by the application renewal operation below, not an OAuth
+refresh-token grant or OpenID Connect login. Lost exchange responses cannot be
+recovered by replaying a code: start a fresh browser attempt. Preserve any previous
+usable native session until the new credential has been durably installed.
+
+## Account read and renewal
+
+`GET /api/auth/native/me` requires exactly one
+`Authorization: Bearer <access_token>` header and no request parameters/body.
+HTTP 200:
+
+```json
+{
+  "account": {
+    "id": "cabinet-account-id",
+    "name": "Display name",
+    "roles": [],
+    "status": "active",
+    "telegramLinked": true,
+    "botChatActive": true,
+    "hasEmail": true,
+    "email": "person@example.test",
+    "googleLinked": false,
+    "pendingEmail": "new-address@example.test",
+    "noTelegram": false,
+    "needsProfile": false,
+    "studentMode": false,
+    "canUseStudentMode": false,
+    "homeHiddenTiles": []
+  },
+  "session": {
+    "id": "nativegrant123456",
+    "expires_in": 7776000,
+    "renew_after": 604801
+  }
+}
+```
+
+The response reads the current account and active grant, never solely token claims.
+Grant identity matches the credential's `session_id`. Account and durations are
+consistent within that operation; separate requests need not share a snapshot.
+For the same current account state, native `account` matches the web's `MeDto`,
+including effective roles/student mode and email omission. The projects verify
+parity for accounts with and without email/pendingEmail, linked login keys, bot
+chat, staff/student-mode states and empty/populated/normalized `homeHiddenTiles`.
+These are account-read cases, not authorization
+for staff actions or a requirement to add profile-edit screens to Daychi.
+
+The [accepted lifetime and renewal policy](#accepted-credential-lifetime-and-renewal)
+governs this wire representation: a new credential's lifetime is
+7,776,000 seconds, and its renewal threshold is strictly greater than 604,800
+seconds. Server time uses whole epoch seconds;
+`renew_after=max(0, issued_at+604801-now)`. At age 604800, `renew_after=1`;
+at age 604801, `renew_after=0`. `expires_in=expiry-now`; credentials are refused
+when `now >= expiry`. No account response is successful at credential expiry.
+
+`POST /api/auth/native/renew` requires the same bearer and UTF-8
+`application/json` body `{}`. Missing, non-object or nonempty bodies and
+unsupported request media types return HTTP 400 `invalid_request`.
+HTTP 200 has the complete code-exchange success shape. Before the renewal threshold
+it returns the supplied credential and its remaining durations. After the threshold
+it returns a fresh credential and fresh durations. The old credential remains valid
+until its own expiry while its grant/account remains eligible. Concurrent renewals
+may issue different credentials belonging to the same grant; neither invalidates
+the others. There is no implicit renewal on account read.
+
+Keeping the predecessor valid makes retry after a lost renewal response or failed
+local write possible. It also means a copied valid bearer can read and renew until
+the grant is revoked, the account is refused or that bearer expires; renewal alone
+does not detect theft. This is an explicit tradeoff of retaining Cabinet's existing
+90-day/seven-day policy with few new mechanisms. Short access credentials with
+rotating refresh credentials or device-bound proofs would be a different profile,
+with different persistence and recovery obligations.
+
+Every native account read and renewal checks credential validity, grant revocation
+and the current account's existence/status. Revocation and issuance must agree:
+no response issued concurrently with an acknowledged revocation may yield a
+credential usable after that acknowledgement. Revocation knowledge is retained
+for as long as any issued credential for that grant could otherwise be accepted.
+
+## Failures and recovery
+
+Protected native operations return only `{"error":"<code>"}` on failure:
+
+The error mapping implements [accepted failure categories](#accepted-failure-categories).
+The `account_blocked` response implements
+[accepted account-block recovery](#accepted-account-block-recovery).
+
+| HTTP | Code | Meaning and client effect |
+|---|---|---|
+| 400 | `invalid_request` | Malformed request; preserve current credential, surface failure |
+| 401 | `invalid_token` | Missing/malformed/expired credential, deleted account or revoked grant; clear only the matching current Cabinet credential |
+| 403 | `account_blocked` | Current Cabinet account blocked; refuse account use, retain credential for possible recovery after unblock |
+| 429 | `rate_limited` | Temporary; preserve credential; `Retry-After` integer 1–3600 seconds |
+| 503 | `temporarily_unavailable` | Temporary server dependency failure; preserve credential |
+| 500 | `server_error` | Temporary unexpected failure; preserve credential |
+
+401 includes `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error="invalid_token"`. A valid browser
+cookie alongside a missing/invalid native bearer cannot change the result.
+An unusable bearer is checked before account status; it cannot reveal block status.
+Token exchange and revocation use their operation-specific 400 errors and the same
+429/500/503 temporary codes. Transport failures, timeouts and invalid success
+bodies are failures, not evidence of sign-out or revocation. No automatic background
+polling is required. Explicit actions, startup and foreground restoration may retry;
+429 delays are respected. Temporary failure can show the last known account as
+cached/unavailable, never authorize an action from that cached account.
+
+## Persistence and sign-out
+
+Daychi stores Cabinet credentials and pending PKCE data in platform secure storage,
+separately from the legacy content credential and separately by issuer. A successful
+new credential is durably stored before sign-in or renewal is reported complete.
+Do not overwrite the previous valid credential on a failed write. This profile
+adds no account picker, in-place account switching or replacement-grant cleanup
+workflow. The retained-credential rule protects interrupted acquisition without
+creating that product requirement.
+
+On restart/foreground, restore only the configured issuer's current credential and
+check `/api/auth/native/me`; if `renew_after=0`, attempt renewal. Failed renewal
+preserves the old valid credential. Responses to superseded requests cannot update
+the current credential/account. Credential storage/read/delete failures are explicit
+local failures, not success, unauthenticated server responses or cleared credentials.
+An already expired stored credential may be discarded without sending it for account
+read; local duration metadata never grants access or extends a server expiry.
+
+`POST /api/auth/native/revoke` accepts form fields `client_id=daychi-native`,
+`token=<native access_token>` and optional `token_type_hint=access_token`.
+No bearer header or browser cookie is required. An authentic issued native token,
+including an expired one whose grant association is still known, revokes its entire
+grant: every credential issued by exchange or renewal becomes unusable. Unknown,
+already revoked or non-native tokens return the same HTTP 200 empty body and cannot
+revoke other grant types. Malformed fields return the exchange's 400 errors.
+Client ID alone cannot identify a grant or revoke an account. Retry is idempotent.
+
+The [accepted local sign-out policy](#accepted-local-sign-out-and-failure-policy)
+governs this storage/revocation wire detail. Sign-out first prevents
+further authenticated app use and invalidates pending
+attempts/responses, then durably deletes the current Cabinet credential, pending
+PKCE transaction and cached Cabinet account. It attempts revocation with the captured
+credential, bounded by the client's request timeout. It must not wait for a network
+response to complete local sign-out. An offline/failed revocation means only local
+sign-out is complete; Cabinet cannot promise remote token invalidation without
+acknowledgement. The client retains no credential for a later revocation queue.
+If secure deletion fails, authenticated use stays disabled in memory and the app
+reports incomplete local sign-out; it cannot claim that restart will remain signed out.
+
+Other native grants and Cabinet browser sessions remain usable. Daychi's existing
+content credential, native invitation flow, schedule cache, ordinary local choices
+and OS reminders remain usable. This profile adds no account-edit synchronization;
+when such pending edits exist under a later contract they belong to Cabinet sign-out
+cleanup, not ordinary local schedule/reminder state.
+
+## Common conformance cases
+
+The [accepted verification scope](#accepted-joint-verification-scope) governs coverage;
+the exact inputs below belong to the accepted wire profile.
+Both contributions use these case IDs and observable results. Server unit/HTTP
+checks may use controlled time and disposable accounts; client checks may use a
+conforming fake provider for interruptions. Joint proof uses a reachable actual
+provider and an actual native development/release build, not Expo Go.
+
+| ID | Input/transition | Required outcome |
+|---|---|---|
+| N01 | Active existing account, PKCE sign-in with and without an existing browser session, account read, app restart; web/native self-profile comparison with email omitted/present, pending email, linked keys, bot chat, staff/student mode and empty/populated/normalized hidden home tiles | Valid browser session returns automatically; necessary login has no additional Cabinet confirmation after completion; same Cabinet account and grant restored; full account matches web MeDto for the same state; legacy content still works |
+| N02 | RFC 7636 verifier `dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk` | Challenge `E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM`; correct exchange succeeds, wrong verifier fails |
+| N03 | Unknown client, altered redirect, malformed state, duplicate query | No redirect to an unregistered URI; no credential installed |
+| N04 | Wrong callback issuer/state/path, added authority/userinfo/port, encoded path, fragment, duplicate decoded fields, code+error | Ignored/refused without URI normalization; current session unchanged; fresh attempt remains possible |
+| N05 | Browser cancel, provider cancel, new attempt, logout; then late callback/response | No stale session resurrection; existing session survives cancellation |
+| N06 | Code at issuance+59 seconds / +60 seconds; replay; concurrent exchange | First valid pre-expiry exchange only; expired/replayed attempts return invalid_grant |
+| N07 | Account blocked/deleted between code issue and exchange | invalid_grant, no usable new grant |
+| N08 | Credential ages 604800 / 604801 seconds; expiry-1 / expiry | No renewal / renewal; valid / invalid_token; stable grant on renewal |
+| N09 | Lost renewal response, crash before persistence or secure write failure after server success | Old credential still usable; retry possible; no false completion |
+| N10 | Lost code-exchange response, crash after dispatch before durable credential installation, duplicate callback or write failure after exchange | No code replay; use a durably installed credential if present, otherwise a fresh browser attempt; prior valid session preserved on failure |
+| N11 | Two native grants plus a browser session; renew first, then revoke using its old token | All first-grant credentials refused; other grant and browser still usable |
+| N12 | Concurrent revoke/renew/read; repeated/unknown-token revocation | No usable credential after acknowledged revoke; repeat/unknown yields 200 |
+| N13 | Block/unblock, account deletion, missing native bearer with browser cookie | 403 retained then recovery / 401 clearing only matching native credential / 401 |
+| N14 | Offline, timeout, 429, 503, malformed success; subsequent recovery | Retain current credential, distinguish unavailable from refused; no background poll requirement |
+| N15 | Offline logout, failed secure deletion, crash during cleanup, delayed request result | Durable local logout when delete succeeds; explicit incomplete logout when it fails; no stale restoration after completed cleanup |
+| N16 | Warm/cold callback; existing quietpractice invitation; separate issuer | Dedicated callback works; legacy invitation works; issuer credentials never mix |
+| N17 | Existing Cabinet login methods, web cookie renewal/logout; Daychi ordinary schedule/reminders/content | Existing behavior retained; no native staff/content privilege introduced |
+
+N01 hidden-home-tile source fixtures absent / `["notice"]` /
+`["events", "payment", "events", "gone"]` produce account values `[]` /
+`["notice"]` / `["payment", "events"]` respectively, equal to the web profile
+for the same account state. The final fixture exercises stored-source normalization;
+unknown keys are not valid wire enum values. Native clients require the declared
+field, array type and keys. New keys require the coordinated shared-profile revision
+already required for changes to account-field meanings.
+
+Token/header leakage checks accompany the cases; test records include outcomes,
+revisions, environment and platform, never real credentials. N01/N11/N15/N16/N17
+need joint/native evidence; server/client interruption cases can supplement it.
+One real supported native platform suffices for the joint integration record;
+callback configuration and client checks cover both supported iOS and Android.
+
+There is no collection selection, ordering, pagination or event subscription in
+this profile: account read returns one current self-account. There is no public
+account lookup, account mutation, device inventory, native push or reminder sync.
+Future incompatible wire/lifecycle changes require a coordinated replacement
+profile; recipients cannot silently select different values. Publication pins this
+document and its common cases to one accessible immutable Workshop revision.
+
+## Standards used for acquisition and transport
+
+[RFC 8252](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8252) informs external-browser native
+authorization; [RFC 7636](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7636) supplies PKCE;
+[RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749) supplies code exchange;
+[RFC 9207](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9207) supplies callback issuer binding;
+[RFC 6750](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6750) supplies bearer transport;
+[RFC 7009](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7009) informs revocation responses.
+[RFC 9700](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9700) informs the threat analysis.
+The application renewal operation and accepted long bearer lifetime are explicit
+local profile choices, not a claim of adopting every OAuth security recommendation.
