+## Prepared next-work proposal — 2026-10-06
+
+The user approved this one-outcome breakdown and minting on 2026-10-06. The
+[native-cabinet-account-session](../tickets/01-0008-native-cabinet-account-session.md)
+ticket is registered after the public pilot, preserving the prior queue order.
+No recipient assignment is published. The scoped source check and impact below
+are the basis of the approved breakdown.
+
+### Scoped source check
+
+Read-only local inspection used Cabinet
+`fc2dee20d64d91122a58e57709c378c5fc91bae7` and Daychi
+`5f9ba442e04cd5dfa70527b9e670b491c491888c` on 2026-10-06. The inspected auth/session
+and native configuration files have no working-tree diff from those revisions.
+This refresh covers the facts below, not every application seam or deployment.
+
+| Boundary | Promise and current evidence | Remaining action |
+|---|---|---|
+| Cabinet account API → native Daychi | [Accepted authority/transport](../boundaries.md#accepted-content-admission): Cabinet owns issuance and accounts; native uses its bearer credential. Cabinet's [guard](https://github.com/gregoryKot/xuanxue-cabinet/blob/fc2dee20d64d91122a58e57709c378c5fc91bae7/api/src/auth/auth.guard.ts) reads a session cookie, checks the current user and blocked status, and renews through Set-Cookie. Its [auth route definitions](https://github.com/gregoryKot/xuanxue-cabinet/blob/fc2dee20d64d91122a58e57709c378c5fc91bae7/shared/src/auth-routes.ts) expose sign-in/account operations, without a native authorization/code exchange. | Add the native acquisition and bearer lifecycle against one agreed contract; preserve existing web behavior. |
+| Cabinet browser sign-in → native return | [Provisional handoff](../boundaries.md#accepted-native-credential-acquisition) uses Cabinet-owned external-browser sign-in with authorization code and PKCE. Daychi's [app configuration](https://github.com/sleontenko/daychi/blob/5f9ba442e04cd5dfa70527b9e670b491c491888c/apps/practice-app/app.json) declares quietpractice; its [native intent handler](https://github.com/sleontenko/daychi/blob/5f9ba442e04cd5dfa70527b9e670b491c491888c/apps/practice-app/src/app/+native-intent.tsx) handles invitation links. An existing scheme is not proof of a Cabinet callback contract. | Agree the exact redirect/client registration and handoff behavior, then implement both ends and demonstrate the return on a native runtime. |
+| Daychi content session versus Cabinet account session | Daychi's [access session](https://github.com/sleontenko/daychi/blob/5f9ba442e04cd5dfa70527b9e670b491c491888c/apps/practice-app/src/features/access/session.ts) stores a Daychi credential in SecureStore, validates its opaque format, and calls Daychi access endpoints. Its current logout waits for that backend before deleting the local credential. Cabinet's [session token](https://github.com/gregoryKot/xuanxue-cabinet/blob/fc2dee20d64d91122a58e57709c378c5fc91bae7/api/src/auth/session-token.ts) is a signed JWT; its current cookie policy is 90-day expiry with [renewal after seven days](https://github.com/gregoryKot/xuanxue-cabinet/blob/fc2dee20d64d91122a58e57709c378c5fc91bae7/api/src/auth/session-renewal.ts). These existing implementations do not settle the native credential contract. | Preserve the existing content-access path while adding Cabinet account access; do not pass a Cabinet token through the existing Daychi token decoder or silently adopt the cookie lifecycle. |
+
+No new auth runtime, sign-in, credential issuance or deployment was exercised.
+The session's complete issue review found no assignment covering this outcome:
+issues 1/2 concern installation, 5/6 concern the public pilot, and 3/4 are withdrawn.
+The remaining shared choices already live in
+[q-0002.0008.0001.0001 — Which authentication standard should Cabinet and Daychi adopt?](q-0002.0008.0001.0001-which-authentication-standard-should-cabinet-and-daychi-adopt.md).
+
+### Approved breakdown
+
+1. **[native-cabinet-account-session](../tickets/01-0008-native-cabinet-account-session.md) — Ready (alignment); HITL.** One coordinating outcome:
+   native Daychi signs in to Cabinet, securely retains/restores its Cabinet-issued
+   credential, reads its current Cabinet account, and signs out of that installation
+   without ending other clients' sessions. Cabinet web access remains supported.
+   This advances the credential row of the accepted migration delta and supplies
+   the account-access foundation for later authenticated schedule/reminder work.
+   It does not claim that the full migration is complete.
+
+   **Responsible contributors:** Workshop owns the shared agreement and joint
+   acceptance; Cabinet's agent/operator owns issuance, bearer validation and web
+   regression proof; Daychi's agent/operator owns the native client and its proof.
+   Cabinet remains the account/session authority and data owner.
+
+   **Dependencies by activity:** local contract preparation has no dependency on
+   public-provider correction, native public-pilot proof or Daychi installation.
+   Breakdown approval and minting are complete. Publishing implementation assignments
+   requires settled shared definitions, common cases and an accessible immutable
+   authority. Each recipient's implementation requires its accepted collaboration
+   installation and its operator's authority. Joint native proof additionally
+   requires the reachable auth provider and a controlled test account. Cabinet's
+   installation is already accepted; Daychi's remains outstanding.
+
+   **Scope:** Cabinet account sign-in, credential restoration/renewal, one existing
+   account read, current-user refusal and temporary-failure recovery, and
+   installation-scoped sign-out. The existing browser/code-and-PKCE handoff remains
+   the provisional baseline. Exact native operations, responses, client/redirect
+   binding, token lifecycle and concurrent credential use remain shared decisions;
+   this proposal selects no token format or expiry policy.
+
+   **Excluded:** Daychi content-admission replacement, content backend retirement,
+   reminder synchronization, protected schedule cutover, gateway implementation,
+   new background polling and native server push. Existing content credentials,
+   ordinary schedule/cache/selections and OS reminders are compatibility obligations.
+
+   **Acceptance:** both projects implement the same pinned native-session contract;
+   a real native runtime completes Cabinet sign-in and account read, restores the
+   session after restart, exercises the agreed expiry/renewal behavior, distinguishes
+   refusal from temporary failure and recovers; sign-out leaves other installations
+   signed in. Cancellation, mismatched/replayed handoffs, unavailable provider and
+   interrupted credential persistence have agreed common outcomes and executed
+   checks. Cabinet web and Daychi's ordinary/content paths have regression evidence.
+   Workshop reviews original-issue reports and reconciles its current records.
+
+### Impact
+
+Load-bearing: this crosses account authority, browser/native transport, credentials
+and their lifecycle. Provider and client must share one contract. The proposed
+single outcome crosses the full path; two recipient issues would be contributions
+to it, not separate horizontal local tickets. It creates a useful account-access
+capability without requiring the whole reminder/content migration.
+
+### Hidden edges
+
+Cookie rolling renewal is not a native renewal response. Cabinet's current logout
+clears a cookie; its stateless token does not establish per-installation server
+revocation. Daychi's existing decoder and storage key belong to a different authority.
+The existing native URL handler handles invitations, so callback integration must
+preserve those links. Refusal, user blocking, renewal races, cancelled browser flows
+and failures during persistence need common outcomes before issue publication.
+The content-admission and gateway straw dogs remain bound to their existing
+questions; this proposal does not resolve or reopen either deferral.
+
+### Leave alone
+
+Existing queue order, readiness/installation assignments, public pilot issues and
+their fixed wire contract; Cabinet web sign-in; Daychi content authority and stored
+credentials; ordinary schedule/cache/reminders; deferred gateway and consolidation.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+**Narrow, then proceed:** prepare one native Cabinet account-session outcome instead
+of one broad authentication/content/reminder migration. The narrowed slice is the
+breakdown above. Its approval adds this outcome to prepared work without releasing
+sibling implementation or finalizing the authentication standard. No further split
+is justified by the inspected path.
+
+### Pending action and resumption
+
+The approved outcome is minted in the ticket linked above. Its next activity is
+alignment of the existing authentication question for this narrow native-session
+scope, followed by complete recipient briefs. No external issue has been created.
+The first unresolved contract decision is the native credential lifecycle: format,
+expiry, renewal, revocation and their observable API behavior. Exact handoff and
+error definitions follow within that same contract. Implementation publication is
+blocked by those definitions, not by the public pilot's required-data correction.
